+#### Masked Opacity Regularization
+- **Definition:** A regularization loss applied to prevent large Gaussians from interfering with visibility tests and causing gradient explosion, by pushing opacity outside a binary mask to zero while leaving opacity inside the mask unconstrained [S2, unofficial].
+- **Pitfall:** Conventional mask regularization (pushing opacity inside mask to one) hurts BRDF parameter training and visibility tests; this method avoids that by only constraining opacity outside the mask [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Regularization in volume rendering emerged as a critical component when moving from simple voxel-based rendering to learned neural representations (NeRFs) and Gaussian splatting. Early NeRF implementations suffered from "floaters" and overfitting to noise, leading to the introduction of noise injection [S1, unofficial] and later, more sophisticated geometric regularizers like Distortion Loss [S1, unofficial][S2, S4, unofficial]. The field has since expanded to include perceptual losses for better visual quality [S1, unofficial], uncertainty modeling for robustness [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial], and specialized losses for dynamic scenes (Forplanes) [S1, unofficial] and explicit Gaussian representations [S1, unofficial]. The evaluation of these methods often relies on metrics that go beyond simple pixel-wise error, such as color-corrected metrics [S1, unofficial] and froxel-based temporal error metrics [S1, unofficial].
+Regularization in volume rendering originated from the need to stabilize the optimization of sparse and implicit representations, where the inverse problem is highly ill-posed. Early works in neural radiance fields (NeRF) introduced noise injection and total variation terms to mitigate the "floaters" artifact [S1, S2, S4, unofficial]. The field has since expanded to include perceptual losses for visual quality [S1, unofficial], geometric constraints for mesh extraction [S1, unofficial], and probabilistic priors for uncertainty estimation [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. Modern approaches often combine multiple regularization terms into a total loss function to balance photometric accuracy with structural integrity [S1, S2, S3][S4, unofficial].
 
